{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-161830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/assets/fd18335a2034613658c65186c4fc6e3e74067e730183d3e66fbacf8ab3601928.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,-y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,R=-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-2x)-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)+(-y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,z,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "334498bc1ad187439245f9602cf2d94ac58a022019cef5a75225ae657db3f22d"} {"question" "q-161830" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161830" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{x}^{2}y+2{y}^{3}-6y"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}y+2{y}^{3}-8y"] ["math" "4y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}"] ["math" "6y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}"] ["math" "8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161830" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/assets/e61f3d7c23ef7876bec2f11c93974551058bcd32f28745898604926d84dbcfee.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨y,0,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cone ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "334498bc1ad187439245f9602cf2d94ac58a022019cef5a75225ae657db3f22d"} {"question" "q-161862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "4x-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x"] ["math" "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-4x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/assets/8a48be0b4c101babf157a05959e923576ad5614113b3eec9e93fe747d5dc3803.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,x,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=x,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,x,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "334498bc1ad187439245f9602cf2d94ac58a022019cef5a75225ae657db3f22d"} {"question" "q-161899" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161899" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{xy-y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161899" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-z,\\,2y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=-z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4,\\,R=2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(0)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,-z,2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "334498bc1ad187439245f9602cf2d94ac58a022019cef5a75225ae657db3f22d"} {"question" "q-161903" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161903" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "2y+\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "4y+\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "y-\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"] ["math" "y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161903" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,-z⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloids $z=1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (-2x)-(0)\\cdot (-2y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-(0)\\cdot (2y)+4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2xy+4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-5\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/state.json" "file_sha256" "f48f8864c1eccb250a19e20d4f17f754724b17fd151771bb35ebb743811e76ca"} {"question" "q-161915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "334498bc1ad187439245f9602cf2d94ac58a022019cef5a75225ae657db3f22d"} {"question" "q-161915" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "valu